## 6. Data & Storage
<!-- ID: data_storage -->
The receipt table is Scribe backend state, not AgentKit shared schema and not a council-local extension. SQLite adds it through the existing `storage/sqlite/schema.py` initialization path. PostgreSQL adds it to the fresh-install `db/init.sql` and through additive numbered migration `005_apply_preview_receipts.sql`. Both define a primary key on token SHA-256 plus indexes on expiry and lifecycle/lease expiry.

Lifecycle is `issued -> applying -> applied|failed_terminal`. An expired applying lease may transition to a new fenced recovery claim. Terminal rows are immutable and replayable until cleanup policy permits removal; cleanup must preserve the non-sensitive audit event. Finalize uses compare-and-set on token hash, applying state, and fence.

Remote parity is server-side atomicity: the remote client exposes the same five methods, the authenticated internal transport allowlist permits them, and the server invokes its configured backend. The client must not fetch then update to simulate claim/finalize. Public-release transport continues to deny backend mutation operations.

Retained intent and target/precondition JSON use the same storage access boundary as managed document state. The opaque token is never stored. Receipt configuration is limited to TTL and claim-lease duration, with a hard 1800-second TTL cap; defaults are owned by the receipt engine rather than duplicated per backend.


